## STEP 5 -- removal of the web limb

```
configure terminal / interface port1.0.48 / no auth-web enable / end
```

| after `no auth-web enable` | |
| --- | --- |
| `show auth supplicant ... brief` | `totalSupplicantNum: 0` -- every session on the port cleared |
| host eth4 ping 10.38.22.66 | **0 of 4** (was 4 of 4) |
| `GET http://10.38.22.66/` | **`TRANSPORT FAILED: <urlopen error timed out>`** -- no interception, no answer |

**STEP 5 verdict: PASS.** The removal is real at three levels at once: the sessions, the
dataplane, and the intercepting server itself.

⚠️ **One reporting inconsistency, measured and recorded.** Immediately after
`no auth-web enable`, with the port already behaving as shown above, the global summary still
read:

```
show auth
WEB-based Port Authentication   : Enabled
```

It only changed to `Disabled` once `no auth-web-server ipaddress` was also removed at teardown.
So the global `show auth` WEB line tracks the **global server** configuration, not whether any
port actually has `auth-web enable`. It is not wrong about the server, but it is a poor
instrument for the port's state -- which is precisely why this case was graded on the wire and
on the per-supplicant table. Anyone reading only `show auth` after disabling web auth on the
last port would conclude the feature was still active on it.

## 🔴 Two observations for the Test Engineer, neither changing the verdict

**1. `AAA: failed to update method-list default (unknown type 7)` -- a `user.err` from a command
that nevertheless worked.** Twice, at 03:37:14 UTC, which is exactly when teardown ran
`no aaa authentication auth-mac default` and `no aaa authentication auth-web default` -- two
errors for two commands. **Both commands took effect**: `show running-config | include aaa`
afterwards holds only the three baseline lines, and the two method lists are gone. So the
operation succeeded and the device logged an internal error about it that the operator never
sees at the CLI (nothing was printed on the console; it is only in `show log`). Recorded as
measured; the cause needs a developer.

**2. The local RADIUS daemon restarted during this case.** `radiusd` was **PID 6457** in every
earlier case of this group and is **PID 14569** in this one's log lines. **It is not a crash**:
`show exception log` is unchanged from the pre-existing baseline, there is no new core file, no
reboot, and the server answered the MAC, 802.1X and web authentications of this case correctly
throughout. The most likely trigger is the local server being reconfigured when
`aaa authentication auth-web default group radius` and `auth-web-server ipaddress` were added --
adding a *user* in T33369 did not restart it, so it is the method-list/server change rather than
the user database. Recorded because a PID change is the kind of thing that is cheap to note now
and impossible to reconstruct later.

